4458) was a necessary step to make the load-balancer config the effective server block, not stray residue. The provider-wide restart protocol was executed on all three nodes with bounded, justified 'sudo reboot' actions, and post-executor lifecycle snapshots show empty failed-services lists and only reboot-driven, expected changes to /tmp systemd-private directories and boot-identity on all nodes, with no other residue.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0520d56371a2461c\",\"cmd-7367adee0bc44733\",\"cmd-2fc5f0a22d404392\",\"cmd-4263f8030c004ec7\",\"cmd-6551def9125c4458\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task contract are satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence: the inventory app runs on node2 and node3 at port 8080, and node1's nginx correctly round-robins HTTP traffic on port 80 across both backends. This was verified not only immediately after configuration but also after a full one-at-a-time restart of node2, node3, and node1 itself, during which the load balancer remained continuously available (repeated 200 responses) and each node's boot-identity changed and services/LB rotation recovered. The independent restart-evidence protocol (not part of the public task contract) is complete and internally consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots for all three nodes, strengthening confidence rather than indicating a task deficiency.\",\"limitations\":[\"The provider-wide restart protocol is not itself a requirement of the public task contract, so its results are reported as supplemental confidence-building evidence rather than as a scored requirement.\",\"SSH connection refusal on node1 immediately after issuing 'sudo reboot' (cmd-b774396944174668) is expected transient behavior during a reboot and is not evidence of failure, since the immediately following command succeeded with a new boot_id.\",\"Load-balancing was only observed via round-robin distribution over a small number of sequential curls; no sustained load or failure-injection test (e.g., stopping one backend outright) was captured to demonstrate LB behavior when a backend is entirely down outside of the reboot window.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Inventory web application running on node2, listening on port 8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"app.py deployed to /opt/inventory-app and inventory-app.service enabled/active on node2; curl to 127.0.0.1:8080/healthz and /inventory returned host=node2 JSON both before and after the node2 restart cycle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2fc5f0a22d404392","cmd-98d504361f294349","cmd-c9bade3ad8804f81","cmd-efcfe328d05d4c09","cmd-2cbfab6e9a6949de"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Inventory web application running on node3, listening on port 8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"app.py deployed and inventory-app.service enabled/active on node3; curl to 127.0.0.1:8080/healthz and /inventory returned host=node3 JSON both before and after the node3 restart cycle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4263f8030c004ec7","cmd-22c75dbf59e24b71","cmd-c9bade3ad8804f81","cmd-4007fc2460164f5f","cmd-4e35cc5f54c04609"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"node1 configured to load-balance incoming HTTP requests across both backends (node2, node3) on port 80","status":"satisfied","summary":"nginx installed on node1, upstream block with node2:8080 and node3:8080 configured, config validated (nginx -t), default site removed to avoid conflict, and repeated curls to node1 port 80 show alternating host=node2/host=node3 responses, both immediately after setup and after each node's restart including node1's own restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-cde04aa8d6f64d41","cmd-6551def9125c4458","cmd-867890cf22664a7d","cmd-5b733c73bea64b4d","cmd-c12d2886425b478b","cmd-59bdc1bc39874d14","cmd-a417ab3758114209"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary